// Fitting Room, the shipped unlock rules.
//
// A dye resolves to its own entry in "dyes" if it has one, else to its
// rarity's entry in "tiers", else to NO RULE. No rule means an empty
// condition list, and an empty condition list is SATISFIED, so anything that
// falls through here is free at level 1 and permanent once a save has stored
// it. Every gap in this file spends a colour rather than withholding one.
//
// WHERE THE NUMBERS COME FROM. Every colour in ../Dyes/eso.json carries an
// "esoUnlock" naming the achievement that awarded it in ESO, and 274 of the
// 306 have one. Nothing evaluates that string. It is the raw material for
// this file, and until 2026-08-02 it was thrown away: five colours were mined
// out of it by hand and the other 301 got their rarity bucket and nothing
// else. That is how all three enchanting rune colours came to be free at
// level 1 while Master Enchanter asked for Enchanting 100.
//
// There is almost nothing to gate by family. 261 distinct achievements across
// 274 colours, and only two of them repeat: Rank 1 to 10 Deconstruction
// Analyst, and Level 10 to 50 Hero. So this is not family gating. Each rule
// below was read off what its own achievement says the character DID and
// mapped to the nearest thing a Skyrim character does. Where the achievement
// carries an intensity word, Apprentice and Expert and Legendary and Master
// and Grand and Seasoned, that word placed it against the other colours on
// the same skill.
//
// RARITY DECIDES WHETHER A COLOUR IS GATED. THE ACHIEVEMENT DECIDES HOW. The
// 64 Commons are the starting palette and the design says so in as many
// words: a level three character with the tier defaults sees the Commons. So
// they keep "always" apart from thirteen, and every Uncommon, Rare and
// Material colour carries a rule of its own instead, bar the three named
// further down that are still waiting on a quest form id.
//
// 220 overrides is a lot for a hand written file. It is also the only thing
// that can break a tier cliff, because a tier is ONE threshold for a whole
// rarity and everything left on it arrives together. Gating by rarity alone
// put 108 colours on level 15 and 84 on level 35, which between them was 89
// percent of everything a character earned after creation, and levels 16 to
// 34 handed out two colours in total.
//
// Measured through the real loaders against the real packs, counting what a
// character holds at each level with every skill at twice their level and the
// deed at zero:
//
//   level     1   5  10  15  20  25  30  35  40  45  50
//   before   66  66  67 174 175 178 179 263 266 266 282
//   after    63  63  68  80  95 115 140 168 213 246 282
//
// No single threshold in here now grants more than 20 colours, against 108 at
// level 15 before. A maxed character who has dyed a hundred channels still
// reaches all 318, and one who has never dyed anything still reaches 282, so
// nothing became unreachable.
//
// AN INTENSITY WORD ORDERS A LADDER, IT DOES NOT SET A NUMBER. ESO spends
// "Master" on a fishing achievement and on the top of the blacksmithing line
// alike, so the word decides where a colour sits among the OTHER colours that
// share its skill and nothing more. Within Smithing that reads Master of
// Refinement 60, Master Deconstructor 75, Master Jewelry Crafter 75, Legendary
// Jewelry Crafter 95, Master Blacksmith 100.
//
// "Legendary" sits below "Master" in the blacksmith and the clothier pair,
// because that is where the first pass already had them. IT DOES NOT REACH THE
// JEWELLERY PAIR, and following it there cost the one ordering a player can
// actually check. Legendary Jewelry Crafter is the Rare colour and Master
// Jewelry Crafter is the Uncommon one, so 80 against 90 sold the rarer swatch
// cheaper than the commoner one, on screen, side by side. In ESO the two words
// are not even on one axis: Legendary is a QUALITY tier and Master is a RANK.
// RARITY OUTRANKS THE INTENSITY WORD wherever the two disagree, because rarity
// is a thing the palette shows and the achievement name is not.
//
// AN ACHIEVEMENT REPEATED ACROSS THE THREE ALLIANCES IS ONE GATE, NOT THREE.
// Pact, Dominion and Covenant Fisherman are the same fishing achievement done
// in three alliances, and 30, 32 and 34 made three colours that always arrive
// together look like a ladder that was walked. They share Alchemy 30.
//
// THE SKILL RULES ARE NOT LEVEL RULES IN DISGUISE. A character who takes
// Smithing to 100 by level 12 has done the thing the smithing colours record
// and gets them at level 12. That is the point: the palette is meant to read
// as where the character has been, not as a second experience bar. Any curve
// measured with skills tracking level is therefore one path through this
// file, and a crafter or a thief walks a different one.
//
// NINE COLOURS ARE DELIBERATELY LEFT ON THEIR RARITY TIERS.
//
//   eso:fighters-guild-red    Associate of the Fighters Guild
//   eso:mages-guild-blue      Student of the Mages Guild
//   eso:dried-blood-brown     Recruit of the Undaunted
//   eso:eyevea-blue           Arch-Mage
//   eso:wolfs-fur-brown       Lycanthropy
//   eso:noxophilic-black      Vampirism
//   eso:lamaes-white          Vampirism Master
//   eso:lycanthrope-gray      Lycanthropy Master
//   eso:soul-shriven-gray     Soul Shriven in Coldharbour
//
// Joining a guild, catching lycanthropy and waking up in Coldharbour are all
// things only a "quest" condition can ask about, and NO QUEST FORM ID IN THIS
// PROJECT HAS BEEN CHECKED AGAINST THE LIVE GAME. An unverified id parses,
// never matches, and locks its colour for the life of the character with no
// log line anywhere, which is worse than the tier they sit on now. They wait
// for a pass that confirms each one in the CK or in xEdit. Six of the nine are
// Common, so they are free at level 1 until that happens; eso:eyevea-blue
// resolves by the Uncommon tier, and eso:lamaes-white and eso:lycanthrope-gray
// by the Rare one.
//
// FOUR MORE COLOURS CARRY THE SAME THEME AND ARE GATED ANYWAY, because
// Fighters Guild Skill Master, Mages Guild Skill Master, Undaunted Skill
// Master and Fighters Guild Veteran name a SKILL LINE rather than a
// membership, and a skill line has an honest Skyrim analogue. Undaunted Skill
// Master sat on the list above until 2026-08-02, filed as a guild because the
// Undaunted are one. The words that decide are "Skill Master", not the name in
// front of them, and its two siblings were already gated at 100.
//
// THE SCHEMA IS CLOSED. "tiers" and "dyes" at the root and nothing else: any
// other top level key fails the whole file, which freezes promotion until it
// is removed. That is the only way a section misspelled "dye" can be caught,
// since a missing section is indistinguishable from one an author never
// wrote. So there is no "_comment" key in here, and none is needed: the
// reader keeps allowComments on, and DyeRulesTests pins five placements of
// real // and /* */ comments as shapes that must keep loading clean.
//
// THE TIER LOOKUP IS AN EXACT BYTE MATCH on the rarity string the dye pack
// carries. "Dye Stamp" has a space in it, and "Dye stamp" or "DyeStamp" here
// would match no tier at all, which frees all 32 of them permanently rather
// than failing anything. tools/check_dye_rules.py refuses that, and refuses a
// skill threshold a new character already meets, because a character starts
// every skill at 15 and racial bonuses reach 25.
//
// "channelsDyed" IS COUNTED BY THE MOD ITSELF, one per channel an Apply
// actually paints, and its name is matched as exactly as a rarity is. A deed
// clause naming a counter nothing produces is NOT rejected: it parses, it
// reads 0 for the rest of that character's life, and the colour it gates never
// unlocks, with no log line anywhere. kDeedChannelsDyed in src/DyeUnlocks.h is
// the only spelling that counts anything, and the same script checks every
// deed name in here against it.
//
// AN OVERRIDE KEY THAT NO DYE CLAIMS IS INERT, not an error, so a typo here
// silently drops its colour onto its rarity tier. Thirteen of the 220
// overrides below sit on Common dyes, and the Common tier is "always". The
// same script checks every key against the packs in ../Dyes, and refuses any
// override weaker than the tier it displaces on a dimension the two share.
//
// AN OVERRIDE MAY NOT ASK FOR LESS THAN ITS OWN TIER, which is why the
// Uncommon tier reads 10 rather than the 15 it used to. The earliest Uncommon
// band is the three starter islands at level 10, and a tier above its own
// lowest band would have had the checker refuse them. One colour resolves by
// that tier now, eso:eyevea-blue, and it comes five levels earlier than it
// did. The Rare tier keeps 35 because 35 is still the earliest Rare band.
{
  "tiers": {
    "Common":    [ { "type": "always" } ],
    "Uncommon":  [ { "type": "level", "min": 10 } ],
    "Rare":      [ { "type": "level", "min": 35 } ],
    "Material":  [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 30 } ],
    "Dye Stamp": [ { "type": "deed", "deed": "channelsDyed", "min": 25 } ]
  },
  "dyes": {
    // The level ladder. "Level N Hero" is the one place ESO states a number,
    // so these five keep the number they were given.
    "eso:novice-tan":      [ { "type": "level", "min": 10 } ],  // Level 10 Hero
    "eso:apprentice-blue": [ { "type": "level", "min": 20 } ],  // Level 20 Hero
    "eso:adept-purple":    [ { "type": "level", "min": 30 } ],  // Level 30 Hero
    "eso:expert-gray":     [ { "type": "level", "min": 40 } ],  // Level 40 Hero
    "eso:master-gold":     [ { "type": "level", "min": 50 } ],  // Level 50 Hero

    // The dyer's own deed, the only counter this mod produces. Black and white
    // are the two colours every palette needs, so they are what using the
    // feature pays for: ten channels for the near black and the near
    // white, a hundred for the pure ones. Their ESO achievements are
    // crafting ones and this is the one place the mapping ignores them on
    // purpose, because the deed is the only condition that can say the
    // player used the dye station at all.
    "eso:obsidian-black": [ { "type": "deed", "deed": "channelsDyed", "min": 10 } ],  // Expert Deconstructor
    "eso:birch-white":    [ { "type": "deed", "deed": "channelsDyed", "min": 10 } ],  // Expert of Refinement
    "eso:void-pitch":     [ { "type": "deed", "deed": "channelsDyed", "min": 100 } ],  // Honor to the Spiritblood
    "eso:marble-white":   [ { "type": "deed", "deed": "channelsDyed", "min": 100 } ],  // Excavation Virtuoso

    // Rank 1 to 10 Deconstruction Analyst, the only real ladder in the data and
    // the ten Material colours. Thirty plus eight a rank, the tenth pulled
    // back to 100 so a Legendary reset still proves it was reached.
    "eso:rank-1-materials":  [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 30 } ],  // Rank 1 Deconstruction Analyst
    "eso:rank-2-materials":  [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 38 } ],  // Rank 2 Deconstruction Analyst
    "eso:rank-3-materials":  [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 46 } ],  // Rank 3 Deconstruction Analyst
    "eso:rank-4-materials":  [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 54 } ],  // Rank 4 Deconstruction Analyst
    "eso:rank-5-materials":  [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 62 } ],  // Rank 5 Deconstruction Analyst
    "eso:rank-6-materials":  [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 70 } ],  // Rank 6 Deconstruction Analyst
    "eso:rank-7-materials":  [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 78 } ],  // Rank 7 Deconstruction Analyst
    "eso:rank-8-materials":  [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 86 } ],  // Rank 8 Deconstruction Analyst
    "eso:rank-9-materials":  [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 94 } ],  // Rank 9 Deconstruction Analyst
    "eso:rank-10-materials": [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 100 } ],  // Rank 10 Deconstruction Analyst

    // THE NINETEEN fr: METALS, the other half of the Material family.
    //
    // They had no rule at all and fell to the tier, so ONE Smithing check at
    // 30 handed all nineteen over at once while the ten above already
    // laddered. That is what this block fixes: the two families now read as
    // one ladder from 30 to 96, and the rungs are set BETWEEN the rank rungs
    // above rather than on top of them, so a Smithing level rarely pays out
    // twice.
    //
    // ⚠ THESE NINETEEN HAVE NO esoUnlock AND THERE IS NO SOURCE MATERIAL FOR
    // THEM. Every colour in ../Dyes/eso.json carries the ESO achievement that
    // awarded it, and the whole method described at the top of this file is
    // reading that string. ../Dyes/pearl.json carries no such field for any of
    // these: they are Fitting Room's own inventions, not ESO imports. So the
    // ladder below is AUTHORED, not derived, and it is commented as authored.
    // Do not read it as if it were mined out of anything.
    //
    // ⚠⚠ ONE REQUIREMENT PER COLOUR. WHERE A COLOUR NAMES A QUEST OR A PLACE,
    // THAT IS THE WHOLE RULE (user 2026-08-11). These ten used to carry a
    // Smithing clause AND the deed, because conditions in one rule are all_of
    // (DyeConditions.cpp) and pairing them with the tier's own dimension let
    // check_dye_rules.py PROVE the override tightened rather than filing ten
    // entries under "taken on trust". That proof was worth having and it is
    // not worth what it costs the player: a locked swatch that recites two
    // lines is asking you to hold two things in your head to understand one
    // colour, and the second line is always the duller of the two. Nobody
    // remembers a colour as "Smithing 82"; they remember it as the one the
    // Brotherhood pays you.
    //
    // What that gives up, stated rather than glossed: on the Smithing axis
    // alone these ten are now a LOOSENING against the Material tier's 30, so
    // check_dye_rules.py reports them as deliberate exceptions instead of
    // proving them. It is not a loosening in practice - a questline is a
    // longer road than a skill level - but the checker cannot know that, so it
    // stays honest about which it can prove. It does enforce the shape:
    // a special clause may not share a rule with a level or a skill.
    //
    // ⚠ EVERY FORM ID BELOW WAS RESOLVED FROM AN EDITOR ID THROUGH
    // tools/data/form_index.json, which make_form_index.py builds by reading
    // the game's own masters. None of them is typed from memory. That matters
    // more here than anywhere else in this file: an unverified id parses,
    // never matches, and locks its colour for the life of the character with
    // no log line - which is exactly why the nine guild colours further up are
    // still waiting on the tier instead. Checking one is cheap now, so these
    // do not wait. (DB09 is 'To Kill an Empire', not the Brotherhood's last
    // quest - that is DB11. Guessing would have locked Black Opal forever.)
    //
    // ⚠ AND IT CHANGES WHAT A MAXED CHARACTER REACHES. The note at the top of
    // this file says a maxed character who has dyed a hundred channels still
    // reaches all 318. That is no longer true and should not be repaired by
    // weakening these: ten of the nineteen now want a questline, so a
    // character who never joined the Brotherhood does not get Black Opal.
    // Unlocks are add-only and travel account-wide, so one character doing the
    // questline hands the colour to every other character on the account - the
    // requirement is per ACCOUNT in practice, which is what makes it fair to
    // ask for a questline at all.
    //
    // Burnished Gold sits at the tier's own 30. It is the tease: the plainest
    // honest metal, reachable exactly when the Material tier used to hand over
    // everything, so the player who reaches Smithing 30 gets ONE metal and
    // eighteen padlocked swatches that each already name what they want.
    //
    // ⚠ THE NINE PLAIN RUNGS STILL LADDER AND THE TEN DEED RUNGS NO LONGER DO.
    // 36 through 88 used to interleave the two, so the numbers rose past the
    // quests. They do not any more: a colour that names a questline is reached
    // when that questline is done and at no particular Smithing level, which is
    // the point of naming it. Read the plain rungs as their own ladder and the
    // ten below as ten separate errands.
    "fr:burnished-gold":  [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 30 } ],  // AUTHORED. The tease: the tier's own number, one metal, no second clause
    "fr:bronze":          [ { "type": "quest", "plugin": "Skyrim.esm", "formId": "01CEF6" } ],  // AUTHORED. C06 Glory of the Dead - the Skyforge is the Companions'
    "fr:beetle-shell":    [ { "type": "locationCleared", "plugin": "Dragonborn.esm", "formId": "0142BB" } ],  // AUTHORED. Kolbjorn Barrow - dug out of the ash, where the beetles are
    "fr:sterling-silver": [ { "type": "quest", "plugin": "Dawnguard.esm", "formId": "007C25" } ],  // AUTHORED. DLC1VQ08 Kindred Judgment - silver is what the Dawnguard answer vampires with
    "fr:rose-gold":       [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 46 } ],  // AUTHORED. Plain rung, between rank 2 and rank 3
    "fr:champagne-frost": [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 48 } ],  // AUTHORED. Plain rung
    "fr:molten-copper":   [ { "type": "locationCleared", "plugin": "Skyrim.esm", "formId": "018EE8" } ],  // AUTHORED. Blackreach - the deepest forge-light in the game
    "fr:moonlit-nacre":   [ { "type": "quest", "plugin": "Dawnguard.esm", "formId": "002853" } ],  // AUTHORED. DLC1VQ07 Touching the Sky - Auriel's bow, and the moon on shell
    "fr:cyan-gleam":      [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 56 } ],  // AUTHORED. Plain rung
    "fr:pure-gold":       [ { "type": "quest", "plugin": "Skyrim.esm", "formId": "0D7D69" } ],  // AUTHORED. TGLeadership Under New Management - the guild's own gold, and its last quest
    "fr:rose-quartz":     [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 62 } ],  // AUTHORED. Plain rung
    "fr:starlight":       [ { "type": "quest", "plugin": "Skyrim.esm", "formId": "01F258" } ],  // AUTHORED. MG08 The Eye of Magnus - the College ends looking at a star
    "fr:bubblegum":       [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 68 } ],  // AUTHORED. Plain rung
    "fr:peacock":         [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 72 } ],  // AUTHORED. Plain rung
    "fr:black-and-blue":  [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 74 } ],  // AUTHORED. Plain rung
    "fr:oil-slick":       [ { "type": "quest", "plugin": "Skyrim.esm", "formId": "04D8D6" } ],  // AUTHORED. DA02 Boethiah's Calling - ebony is the oily black one
    "fr:black-opal":      [ { "type": "quest", "plugin": "Skyrim.esm", "formId": "01EA59" } ],  // AUTHORED. DB11 Hail Sithis! - the Brotherhood's last quest, not DB09
    "fr:dragonfly-wing":  [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 88 } ],  // AUTHORED. Plain rung
    "fr:abyssal-pearl":   [ { "type": "quest", "plugin": "Dragonborn.esm", "formId": "0179D7" } ],  // AUTHORED. DLC2MQ06 At the Summit of Apocrypha - the deepest thing in the palette

    // Smithing: forging, refining, traits, styles, jewellery.
    "eso:nirncrux-red":             [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 40 } ],  // Learn the Nirnhoned Trait
    "eso:weathered-keystone-green": [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 45 } ],  // Mining Tunes
    "eso:moonstone-white":          [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 50 } ],  // Trait Master
    "eso:superior-blue":            [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 55 } ],  // Superior Attire
    "eso:polished-steel-gray":      [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 60 } ],  // Master of Refinement
    "eso:dwarven-brass":            [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 65 } ],  // Dwemer Style Master
    "eso:epic-violet":              [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 68 } ],  // Epic Attire
    "eso:motif-master-brown":       [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 72 } ],  // Rare Style Master
    "eso:craftmaster-gold":         [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 75 } ],  // Master Deconstructor
    "eso:earthforge-iron-gray":     [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 75 } ],  // Legendary Blacksmith
    // THE JEWELLERY PAIR RUNS THE OTHER WAY ROUND TO THE REST. The Uncommon
    // colour is the cheaper one and the Rare colour is the dearer one, which
    // is the only ordering a player holding both can actually see. It read
    // Master 90 against Legendary 80 until 2026-08-02, on the "Legendary sits
    // below Master" precedent the blacksmith and clothier pairs set. That
    // precedent does not reach this pair: in ESO, Legendary is a QUALITY tier
    // and Master is a RANK, so the two words are not on one axis here, and
    // following them put the Rare below the Uncommon.
    "eso:antique-silver":           [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 75 } ],  // Master Jewelry Crafter
    "eso:divine-gold":              [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 95 } ],  // Legendary Jewelry Crafter
    "eso:smith-steel-blue":         [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Smithing", "min": 100 } ],  // Master Blacksmith

    // Enchanting: the three rune types, then the master.
    "eso:aspect-red":          [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Enchanting", "min": 30 } ],  // Aspect
    "eso:potency-blue":        [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Enchanting", "min": 40 } ],  // Potency
    "eso:essence-green":       [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Enchanting", "min": 50 } ],  // Essence
    "eso:enchanter-tangerine": [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Enchanting", "min": 100 } ],  // Master Enchanter

    // Alchemy: gathering, cooking, brewing, poisons, and every fishing
    // achievement, which has no closer Skyrim verb than filling a satchel.
    //
    // THE THREE ALLIANCE FISHERMEN SHARE ONE GATE. Pact, Dominion and Covenant
    // Fisherman are the same achievement in three alliances, so 30 and 32 and
    // 34 was three simultaneous unlocks dressed up as a ladder: no character
    // ever sat between them long enough to notice an order. One honest
    // threshold says what the three of them actually are.
    "eso:pact-fisher-red":          [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Alchemy", "min": 30 } ],  // Pact Fisherman
    "eso:dominion-fisher-yellow":   [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Alchemy", "min": 30 } ],  // Dominion Fisherman
    "eso:covenant-fisher-blue":     [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Alchemy", "min": 30 } ],  // Covenant Fisherman
    "eso:nibenay-trout-green":      [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Alchemy", "min": 50 } ],  // Cyrodiil Angler
    "eso:recipe-reaper-red":        [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Alchemy", "min": 55 } ],  // Recipe Compendium
    "eso:viridian-venom":           [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Alchemy", "min": 65 } ],  // Toxicologist
    "eso:slaughterfish-flesh-pink": [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Alchemy", "min": 65 } ],  // Master Fisher
    "eso:provisioner-brown":        [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Alchemy", "min": 70 } ],  // Master Provisioner
    "eso:nirnroot-gray":            [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Alchemy", "min": 80 } ],  // Botanist
    // Brewing is alchemy, not rhetoric. This one sat on Speech 100 from the
    // first pass, inherited rather than read off Master Chef, and it is the
    // only rule in here the esoUnlock sweep did not author.
    "eso:brewers-ale-brown":        [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Alchemy", "min": 100 } ],  // Master Chef
    "eso:kresh-flower-red":         [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Alchemy", "min": 100 } ],  // Grand Master Crafting Harvester
    "eso:alchemist-bilious":        [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "Alchemy", "min": 100 } ],  // Master Alchemist

    // Armour: the clothier line, and the two colours a tank earns.
    "eso:ironweed-violet":   [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "LightArmor", "min": 75 } ],  // Legendary Clothier
    "eso:clothier-purple":   [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "LightArmor", "min": 100 } ],  // Master Clothier
    "eso:brassy-brass":      [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "HeavyArmor", "min": 80 } ],  // Seasoned Construct Slayer
    "eso:adamantine-silver": [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "HeavyArmor", "min": 100 } ],  // Dragonstar Arena Conqueror

    // Weapons. Each of the six creature lines pairs a plain Slayer with a
    // Seasoned one; the plain half is Common and stays free, so only the
    // Seasoned half is gated.
    "eso:brigand-brown":      [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "OneHanded", "min": 80 } ],  // Seasoned Humanoid Slayer
    "eso:blademasters-mauve": [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "OneHanded", "min": 85 } ],  // Maelstrom Arena Champion
    "eso:syffim-red":         [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "OneHanded", "min": 100 } ],  // Fighters Guild Skill Master
    "eso:stendarr-white":     [ { "type": "skill", "skill": "TwoHanded", "min": 75 } ],  // Master Monster Slayer
